                <pb facs="0549" n="545" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg545"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6661: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the United
                        Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kennedy</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-08-10T17:00:00-04:00">August 10, 1938—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>437. In the light of your 710, August 3, 10 a.m.,<anchor n="19" xml:id="d556-f1"
                    /> and your mail despatch No. 860, July 28, just received, the Department
                    assumes that, notwithstanding the British Government’s decision reported in your
                    640, July 15, 2 p.m., there may be under consideration proposals which would
                    involve assistance by the Exports Credit Guarantee Department, as indicated in
                    UP press dispatch from London of July 31. The Embassy will of course keep the
                    Department currently and fully informed of any developments in this
                    connection.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
